 \section*{Abstract}
 \addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Abstract}
 
-I built and tested a USB-C laboratory power supply using an earlier DTU charge-pump power-factor-correction (PFC) resonant converter and supplied planar magnetics. My work was to integrate that starting point into working hardware and develop its controller. The main control problem came from the detachable load: when demand disappeared, energy remained in the resonant path and capacitors. I needed to regulate the loaded supply and limit further charging at no load.
+I built and tested a USB-C laboratory power supply using an earlier DTU charge-pump power-factor-correction (PFC) resonant converter and supplied planar magnetics. I integrated it into working hardware and developed the controller. The detachable load created the main control problem: when demand disappeared, energy remained in the resonant path and capacitors. I needed to regulate the loaded supply and limit further charging at no load.
 
 I kept the carrier fixed at a nominal \SI{1}{\mega\hertz} and used the STM32G071 to adjust energy transfer through the GaN half bridge's burst-enable interval. I moved feedback acquisition to circular direct memory access (DMA) so analog reads would not block the control loop. The final PWR-1.9.20 controller regulates the isolated secondary bulk bus, uses limited precharge while feedback starts, and applies different duty limits under load and at no load. A downstream output stage provides the tested \SI{5}{\volt}, \SI{9}{\volt}, \SI{15}{\volt}, and \SI{20}{\volt} USB-C settings.
 
-At a logged input of \SI{240}{\volt}, \SI{50}{\hertz}, the matched \SI{20}{\volt} measurements gave \SIrange{73.97}{79.89}{\watt} at the load. Average output voltage was \SIrange{19.973}{19.991}{\volt}, with recorded peak-to-peak ripple of \SIrange{0.416}{0.452}{\volt}. Logged power factor was 0.97--0.99 and current total harmonic distortion was \SIrange{2.23}{3.98}{\percent}. The input--output pairs give a screening efficiency of \SIrange{76.5}{77.1}{\percent}; uncertain timing and calibration limit the accuracy of that estimate. The no-load and load-removal captures show the controller operating through the tested events. A thermal image labeled \SI{20}{\volt}, \SI{4}{\ampere} showed a \SI{90.4}{\celsius} surface maximum. These results make loss measurement, heat removal, and timed protection tests the next priorities.
+At a logged input of \SI{240}{\volt}, \SI{50}{\hertz}, the matched \SI{20}{\volt} measurements gave \SIrange{73.97}{79.89}{\watt} at the load. Average output voltage was \SIrange{19.973}{19.991}{\volt}, with recorded peak-to-peak ripple of \SIrange{0.416}{0.452}{\volt}. Logged power factor was 0.97--0.99 and current total harmonic distortion was \SIrange{2.23}{3.98}{\percent}. The input--output pairs give a screening efficiency of \SIrange{76.5}{77.1}{\percent}; uncertain timing and calibration limit the accuracy of that estimate. The no-load and load-removal captures show the controller operating through the tested events. A thermal image labeled \SI{20}{\volt}, \SI{4}{\ampere} showed a \SI{90.4}{\celsius} surface maximum. The next priorities are to measure losses, improve heat removal, and test protection timing.
 
-I then document the V2 STM32F410 implementation, PWR-2.0.1 firmware, and browser control and logging tools. The appendices include the full controller and supporting source. The revised July--October plan connects this development to a V2 retest, diagnosis of the power shortfall, and a V3 edge-card design with a common power-density comparison method. Powered V2 characterization and the V3 comparison remain open.
+I also document the V2 STM32F410 implementation, PWR-2.0.1 firmware, and browser control and logging tools. The appendices include the full controller and supporting source. The revised July--October plan calls for a V2 retest, diagnosis of the power shortfall, and a V3 edge-card design. It also calls for a common method for comparing power density. Powered V2 characterization and the V3 comparison remain open.
 
-I demonstrated digitally controlled operation at approximately \SI{80}{\watt}. The revised plan keeps a \SI{100}{\watt} continuous target and a \SI{65}{\watt} continuous minimum. Establishing either rating needs defined-duration thermal and stability tests. Full-range zero-voltage switching, USB-PD conformance, safety, and EMC also need dedicated verification. The next step is to identify what limits sustained output, measure the effect of each controlled change, and use those findings to guide V3.
+I demonstrated digitally controlled operation at approximately \SI{80}{\watt}. The revised plan keeps a \SI{100}{\watt} continuous target and a \SI{65}{\watt} continuous minimum. Either rating requires thermal and stability tests of defined duration. Full-range zero-voltage switching, USB-PD conformance, safety, and EMC also need dedicated verification. The next step is to identify what limits sustained output, measure the effect of each controlled change, and use those findings to guide V3.
